- op: discuss  # ddc5
  at: '2026-08-04T05:07:46Z'  # ddc5
  by: agent  # ddc5
  actor: jgstern_agent  # ddc5
  clock: 3  # ddc5
  nonce: ddc5  # ddc5
  message: "SATISFIED. PR #196, merged.\n\nBEHAVIOURAL EVIDENCE -- the item's own filed repro, re-run:\n  kserve, two runs of the identical binary, same claims file, findings keyed\n  on (source_symbol, sink_symbol, taint_label):\n    flows whose path differs between the two runs: 0   (filed value: 2 of 182)\n\nCAUSE CONFIRMED, having been filed as suspected. Direct probe on\npropagate_taint_structural with eight equally-valid middle hops:\n  PYTHONHASHSEED=7 twice        -> identical path both times\n  PYTHONHASHSEED=1,2,3,4,5      -> midA, midF, midE, midF, midC\nSo set-iteration order over str keys, seeded per process, was exactly it.\n\nFIX: candidate (a) from the filing -- a declared tie-break. BFS visits each\nnode's callees in sorted id order, so the witness is a deterministic function\nof the input rather than of the interpreter's hash seed. Two lines, both in\n_reachability_past_sanitizers (the sanitizer-barrier refactor in PR #195 had\nalready collapsed the two propagators' duplicate walks into one helper, which\nis why this is not four sites).\n\nAND THE FILING'S WARNING WAS HONOURED. It said: \"Do not do (a) and describe it\nas 'the path', which would be the same silent-arbitrary-choice shape with a\nstable coat of paint.\" TaintFlowFinding.path's docstring now states that it is\nONE witness among possibly several equally-valid routes, that the others are\nreal and not listed, that the choice is a declared tie-break rather than an\nartifact, and that a consumer needing \"every route\" must not read this field\nas if it were that. I did not add a count-of-alternatives field -- candidate\n(b) proper -- and am not claiming to have; the semantics are now stated, not\nenumerated.\n\nSCOPE, which the filing left UNMEASURED and asked to be measured first:\n * Does the structural propagator share the shape? YES -- the probe above IS\n   propagate_taint_structural. Both propagators share _build_adjacency and now\n   share the fixed walk.\n * Any other BFS-derived field reporting an arbitrary witness? Repo-wide sweep\n   for set-iterating traversals found exactly one other site, compact.py's\n   component-root accumulation, and it is order-INDEPENDENT: it accumulates\n   into a set and reads only len(). No other arbitrary-witness field found.\n   That is a sweep result, not a guarantee about code shapes the grep pattern\n   would not match.\n\nTESTS: an in-process test asserting the SPECIFIC declared winner (a proxy, but\none that carries coverage), a non-vacuity floor proving the fixture really\noffers eight competing witnesses, and a subprocess test running five hash\nseeds and asserting one distinct result -- the last contributes no coverage by\nconstruction and exists because it is the property itself rather than a proxy\nfor it.\n\nWHAT THIS COST BEFORE IT WAS FOUND: the first WI-kabif A/B read as \"30 flows\nappeared and disappeared\" purely because the comparison keyed on path. The\nflows had not moved at all."  # ddc5
